    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>SystÃ¨me de cote ÃnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="5" build="8">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.5b8</English>
                <French>v15.5b8</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="true" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="3" coolingSeasonMonths="0" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="true" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="true">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="21" heatingNighttime="21" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="false" heatedGarage="true" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="3" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is average for air leakage, there are however some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors upstairs, and along the baseboards along the wall in the washroom upstairs (towards the corner behind the toilet). 



I recommend you install an electrical outlet/switch gasket behind the plastic faceplates of any electrical outlets/switches located on an exterior wall. These gaskets will help eliminate the air leakage coming around the installed boxes by creating a seal between the gasket and drywall. 



The exterior doors upstairs could use new weather stripping to ensure a good seal is created. As you mentioned potentially replacing these doors, that would be a great time to do so. See my comments below regarding the door replacements. 



For the area along the upstairs bathroom wall, I suggest removing the baseboard to examine a potential solution. If there appears to be enough room, I recommend using low expansion sprayfoam to fill in the gap. However, if there is not much of a gap, an interior grade silicone can be used along the floor and wall joining wood members. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 100&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;







&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) of extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior of the walls. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R20 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,150&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;36.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was estimated to have 10 inches (R27) of blown fiberglass insulation. I have modelled adding 7 inches (R25) of blown cellulose insulation overtop of the existing blown fiberglass, to achieve R52 in the attic. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 7 inches (R25) blown cellulose insulation to the attic (for a total of R52), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 210&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your slab-on-grade floor appears to be 4 inches of concrete, with no insulation. Poured-concrete foundations can be insulated from either the outside or inside, providing there are no serious water or structural problems. Preserved-wood foundations, made with sheathing and studs, are generally insulated by filling in the stud space. Slab-on-grade foundations are typically insulated on the exterior edge. Occasionally, they are insulated on top of the slab and under the floor finish.



I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of extruded polystyrene between the poured slab-on-grade and flooring. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R10 of extruded polystyrene insulation to the conrete floor, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 380&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</foundation>
                <doors>

As you mentioned replacing your upstairs exterior doors with fiberglass ones, as well as your back door appears to be slightly warped and does not quite seal completely when closed, I have modelled replacing these exterior doors with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified fiberglass door rated for climate zone 3.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing these doors with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified doors rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 150&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</doors>
                <HeatingSystem>

If you decide to install an CSA certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, as well as a dedicated combustion air duct. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings (with current oil furnace):&lt;/b&gt; 

By installing an CSA certified pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 650 &lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;





&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.













&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist&lt;/b&gt;

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



&lt;font color="lightseagreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
